  "markdown": "A prime residence in central London\n\n# How we solve imperfect arches: bespoke external-corner trim for wallcoverings\n\nAn arch that was not true, finished in wallcovering with a specialist external-corner profile bent to its curve and the paper scribed into the trim, so the edge stays crisp and does not wear or lift.\n\n![A wallcovered arch seen from below, its curved outer edge finished as one crisp, even line, framing a room with a decorative plaster ceiling and a chandelier.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-01-800.webp)\n\n## Project record\n\nClient, materials & project facts\n\n**Client**\n: Private client\n\n**Designer**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Main contractor**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Maker and paper**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Area or rooms**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Scope**\n: Wallcovering installation and specialist edge detailing\n\n**Year**\n: 2019\n\nHow do you get a clean wallcovering edge on an arch that is not true? You stop asking the paper to make the edge. We surveyed the arch, ordered a specialist external-corner profile made for wallcoverings, bent it to the curve, fixed it, and scribed the paper into the trim’s return. The arch now reads as one crisp, even line, and its most exposed edge is protected rather than left bare.\n\n## At a glance\n\n- **Where:** a prime residence in central London\n- **The detail:** the curved external edge of a wallcovered arch\n- **The difficulty:** an arch, and an arch edge, that were not true\n- **The method:** survey, a specialist external-corner profile ordered for the job, bent to the arch, fixed, and the paper scribed into its return\n- **Photographed:** May 2019\n- **Our role:** wallcovering installation and the edge detail\n- **Photographs:** the finished arch first, then finished rooms and details in the same residence. We do not name the home or its owner, and window views and personal items are blurred.\n\n## The problem\n\nAn arch is the least forgiving edge in a room. The eye follows its curve from one spring point to the other, so any flat spot, kink or wander in the line shows at once, and wallcovering wrapped over the edge follows every one of them.\n\nThis arch was not true. The curve did not run evenly, and the plastered edge along it was not a clean, consistent line. Hanging the paper straight over it would have recorded those faults in the finish.\n\nThere was a second problem: durability. A bare paper edge on an external curve is fragile. It is the part of the wall most likely to be brushed by shoulders, bags, furniture and cleaning, and on a curve the paper is already working as it turns the corner. Left exposed, an edge like that scuffs, frays, wears through and, in time, lifts.\n\n## The solution\n\n1. **Survey.** Before anything was hung, we surveyed the arch: the line of the curve on each face, the condition of the edge, and where it departed from a true line. The survey decided the approach: a fixed, true edge for the paper to finish against, rather than a bare wrap that would show the faults.\n2. **A specialist profile, ordered for the job.** We ordered a specialist external-corner profile made for wallcoverings for this arch. Profiles of this kind are slim, flexible corner sections designed to sit at the arris and give the wallcovering a hard, consistent edge to meet.\n3. **Bent to the arch.** The profile was bent to the arch’s curve and set so that it describes one smooth line from spring point to spring point, rather than following the faults of the old edge.\n4. **Fixed.** The profile was fixed along its whole length, so it holds its line and does not move as the paper dries or the room settles.\n5. **Scribed in.** The wallcovering was then hung on each face and scribed into the trim’s return: cut to the line of the profile, so the edge of the paper is held and protected by the trim instead of sitting exposed on the corner.\n\n## The result\n\nA crisp, durable, high-end edge. The arch reads as a single clean curve, the paper finishes to a precise line on both faces, and the most exposed edge in the room is protected by the profile rather than left to wear. The edge is designed to keep its line instead of scuffing and lifting.\n\n## The type of trim used\n\nWe do not name the exact product used on this arch. The profile was of the type shown in these two manufacturers’ examples, which are cited for the type of trim, not as the product installed here:\n\n- **Arte, [Profilo corner guards](https://www.arte-international.com/en/profilo-corner-guards).** A corner guard for outside corners and arches, adjustable from 90° to 180°. The wallcovering is applied to the raised part of the guard and the surplus cut away, and the guard can be painted to match the wallcovering.\n- **Tarkett, [ProtectWall flexible set-in corner](https://professionals.tarkett.com/en_EU/collection-C001470-protectwall-set-in-corners/set-in-cor-pvc-flex-white).** A flexible PVC outside corner, glued in place and installed under the wall covering, designed to follow uneven corners.\n\nWhich profile suits a given arch depends on the wallcovering, its thickness, the radius and the wear the edge will take. That is a decision for the survey, not for a catalogue.\n\n## When this detail is worth specifying\n\n- **Arches, curved reveals and rounded nibs** where the edge is out of true and rebuilding it is not in the programme.\n- **Edges that take traffic:** hallways, stair openings, hotel corridors and anywhere a shoulder or a trolley passes close.\n- **Delicate or textured wallcoverings,** such as natural weaves and silks, whose cut edge frays or wears quickly if left exposed.\n\nPreparing the wall is the other half of a clean edge: our [wall preparation guide](https://www.mrwallcover.com/advice/wall-preparation/) covers what we check before anything is hung, and our guide to [bubbles and lifting edges](https://www.mrwallcover.com/advice/wallcovering-problems-and-aftercare/) explains what to do if an edge does start to go. If you have an arch, a reveal or a curved corner in mind, [send us photographs and the wallcovering reference](https://www.mrwallcover.com/contact/) and we will advise on the detail at survey.\n\n## Photographs\n\n![Finished bedroom with a pale textured wallcovering, geometric panelling with brass inlay behind the bed, and a crystal chandelier.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-02-800.webp)\n\n![Finished drawing room with pale textured walls, a gold geometric pendant, a buttoned leather ottoman and velvet seating in green and magenta.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-03-800.webp)\n\n![The drawing room towards its joinery wall, with a pale wallcovering behind a group of round wall mirrors and a green velvet sofa.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-04-800.webp)\n\n![Upright view of the drawing room: pale textured walls beneath an ornate plaster cornice and ceiling rose.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-05-800.webp)\n\n![A cloakroom lined in a fine horizontal-weave wallcovering, with a black marble basin and a backlit mirror.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-06-800.webp)\n\n![A round brass mirror above a glass-topped chest of drawers, against a pale textured wallcovering.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-07-800.webp)\n\n![Close detail of a finished external edge: a fine woven wallcovering runs straight and tight to a crisp corner line.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-08-800.webp)\n\n![Close detail of a woven wallcovering on two neighbouring surfaces, each edge cut clean either side of a narrow shadow gap.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-09-800.webp)\n\nAll 2 more photographs from this project\n\n![Close detail of a textured wallcovering cut neatly around a wall-mounted lighting control.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-10-800.webp)\n\n![Round wall mirrors with gold radiating frames against a pale wallcovering.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/arch-trim-11-800.webp)\n\nHow the commission ran\n\n## The same standards, on every commission.\n\nEach commission has a written scope by room, walls prepared until they are true, protected floors, batch and shade control, set-out from the room’s focal points, and a return visit. [Read the method](https://www.mrwallcover.com/practice/#method).\n\nThe six standards in full\n\n01\n\n### Survey and scope, room by room\n\nWe measure the wall that will actually be hung, allow for the repeat and the wastage, and write a scope of works room by room before anyone orders paper.\n\n02\n\n### Preparation decides the finish\n\nWalls are filled, made good and primed until they are true. If a wall is not ready for the material specified, we say so before the last drop is ordered.\n\n03\n\n### Protection and containment\n\nFloors, furniture and finished joinery are protected as part of the hang. Work is contained and the place is left tidy each evening. In a home we are visitors; in a hotel we work around the house.\n\n04\n\n### Batch and shade control\n\nDeliveries are checked against the measure, and batches and shading are managed so that a corridor of rooms reads as one surface.\n\n05\n\n### Set out from the room's focal points\n\nPattern is set out from chimneypieces, doorcases and bed walls, so the pattern sits square to the cornice and the joinery.\n\n06\n\n### One team, then the return visit\n\nThe people who start are the people who finish. Four to six weeks after completion we come back to look at every seam and edge, and the workmanship is guaranteed for twelve months.\n\n[See the finish up close: seams, internal corners, edges and joins from our own installations](https://www.mrwallcover.com/details/)\n\nBy [Dorin Burcus](https://www.mrwallcover.com/about/#dorin), founder · Last updated 10 October 2026\n",
